The extraction scripts use the pinned `osmium` dependency declared in their script metadata. `extract_roofs.py` writes a single JSON list of supported shapes and relevant tags. `extract_trees.py` writes an overview GeoJSON, a zoom-14 manifest and tiles. `extract_street_detail.py` writes a zoom-14 manifest and tiles. Tree and street output directories must be empty when rebuilt. To replace a region's assets, retain the relevant source and checksum, update its configured files, then restart the server so its asset fingerprint refreshes SQLite.

== Building extrusions and roof replacement

The MapLibre style's `building-3d` layer uses tile `render_height`, `render_min_height` and `colour` attributes when available. Pure red colors (`red`, `#f00`, `#ff0000`, case-insensitive) are displayed as muted warm stone (`#b09b8b`); all other colors retain the original rendering and fallback behavior. `roof-layer.mjs` loads the region's prepared roof file on demand at zoom 15 or above, then compares extracted OSM IDs to visible building source features. The implementation maps a way ID to suffix-derived tile IDs `id*10` or `id*10+2`, and a relation ID to `id*10+3`. These conventions were checked against a Toronto tile; provider changes or mismatches, including unverified New York matching, can prevent replacement. Ambiguous matches are not applied.

`buildRoofParts` checks geometry and height, derives building base and height from OSM tags, tile render properties or level-based fallback, and samples terrain at the roof footprint. For a supported pitched form, it creates Three.js wall and roof geometry and hides the original tile extrusion only after replacement geometry is ready. A flat roof with a mapped color or material uses a thin color overlay and retains the native building. Unsupported or invalid geometry, missing height, unmatched IDs, failed loading or unresolved elevation leave the native flat extrusion in place.

`roof-geometry.mjs` supports skillion, gabled, hipped, pyramidal, dome, cone and onion shapes; extraction maps `shed` to skillion and `pitched` to gabled. Direction can come from numeric or compass `roof:direction`, or orientation and an inferred minimum-area footprint frame. Roof height is taken from a valid explicit height, levels or angle when available, then from a bounded estimate. Concave skillion and gabled footprints are handled; flat and skillion polygons can retain holes. Hipped geometry is limited to suitable four-corner footprints, and unsupported holes or concavity for other pitched shapes fall back. Colors are parsed from supported OSM color names or hex values, or selected from the original material palette and fallback colors. A parsed pure red (`#ff0000`) is replaced by muted warm stone (`#b09b8b`), matching the MapLibre treatment; other colors and source tags remain unchanged.

== Tree extraction and rendering

`extract_trees.py` records OSM `natural=tree` nodes and samples `natural=tree_row` ways. A row uses a mapped count if available; otherwise it uses the mapped spacing or an 8 m default. Sampling is deterministic and capped at 1,000 points per row. Estimated points within approximately 2 m of an already retained tree point are suppressed. Records carry longitude, latitude, height, crown diameter, needleleaf flag and whether the position was estimated; the manifest records counts and tile keys.

Below zoom 15, the overview GeoJSON is a clustered source, but only unclustered points are drawn. Mapped and estimated positions have different colors. At zoom 15 and above, `tree-layer.mjs` selects visible zoom-14 tiles, prioritizes tiles nearest the map center and limits concurrent fetches to eight. The cache evicts inactive tiles when it exceeds 48 entries; active tiles are retained. Valid tree heights range from 2-60 m and crown diameters from 1-40 m; otherwise height defaults to 8 m and crown diameter to the larger of 3 m or 55% of height. Three.js instanced meshes draw schematic trunks, broadleaf or needleleaf crowns, with deterministic color variation and additional nearby canopy detail.

== Street detail, selection and lighting

Street extraction records OSM `highway=street_lamp` and `amenity=bench` nodes as location and type. From zoom 17, the street layer loads visible zoom-14 tiles, permits up to eight pending requests, and evicts inactive cache entries above 48. It selects records in the view within approximately 1.2 km of the map center, sorts by distance and retains at most 1,000. Three.js instancing renders shared schematic lamp and bench geometries; dimensions and bench orientation are illustrative. Instances with unavailable terrain elevation are omitted while terrain is enabled.

For ordinary map clicks, `featureDetails` extracts a building's available render height, and a schematic Three.js selection marker is positioned at that height plus ground elevation. Network and NYC reservation hits are handled first. This is a selection marker; the current click path does not create a general OSM attribute-inspection panel.

Lighting is fixed to the southeast by the application composition. MapLibre uses a map-anchored light; custom Three.js scenes use a hemisphere light and a directional light with the matching preset. When enabled at zoom 15 or above and buildings are visible, `shadowFeatures` projects each eligible footprint away from the light, considering at most 1,500 input polygons and capping their render height at 150 m. The resulting ground polygons do not model time of day, terrain occlusion, building-to-building shadows, courtyards, tree shadows or tile completeness.

== Regional network adapters

The Toronto adapter loads TELUS and Bell GeoJSON, validates feature structure and counts, and derives map markers, direction arrows and fans. All enabled licensed markers are retained independently of overview fan eligibility. Eligible outdoor purpose-built towers can have a schematic model based on consistent reported heights. The NYC adapter validates and loads its reservation points, filters proposed reservations from the map, and keeps them in the searchable list. It does not synthesize radio attributes.

= Failure handling and operational boundaries

- Region-catalog failure hides the loading overlay and exposes a reload action. Map-style failure exposes the map error UI. Terrain failure is reported separately; terrain can be disabled.
- Tree, roof and street-detail load failures display status and instruct the user to toggle the affected control to retry. Search failures are shown in the search panel; the browser times out the Photon request after 10 seconds.
- Network data is validated before use and can be retried by toggling the layer. Project data loading rejects invalid datasets or saved-list formats; the user can reselect a project to retry.
- A failed project write leaves the existing project list and boundary intact. If the selected project changes while a write is pending, a successful response still updates the saved project's in-memory cluster; a detached failed popup is reported in the project status.
- The SQLite database is local mutable state, not an asset cache that can be discarded without losing project edits. Multiple live servers pointed at different database paths can diverge. No cross-database synchronization, user access control or planning-site audit/recovery UI is implemented.

= Validation and source traceability

`make.ps1` is a Windows PowerShell entry point for the six Makefile targets. It runs from the repository root, uses the same uv, Node and npm commands and propagates failures; its test and syntax lists must be maintained alongside the Makefile. Tests use the uv-managed interpreter for JavaScript-driven Python processes unless `PYTHON` is set. Before test-ui, it checks the npm packages and launches the selected headless browser; if unavailable, it runs npm ci or installs that browser. Downloads require network access. On Windows, the browser suite defaults to one worker because test files share a planning database. The server explicitly serves JavaScript and module files as `text/javascript` regardless of Windows file associations. Windows registry files use inherited filesystem ACLs; the POSIX mode-bit test is skipped on Windows. The experiment worker uses a nonblocking byte-range lock on Windows and flock on Unix so only one local worker owns a registry.

`make check` runs the JavaScript and Python tests and syntax checks, including view-model state and dependency-boundary tests. `make test-ui` runs the Playwright suite against a local server with a controlled map style and mocked external services; the live tests require `NDT_LIVE_UI=1`. The project tests cover default selection, full-radius geometry and hull containment, site edits, saved-list behavior, failure and retry, boundary fitting and region-specific limitations. SQLite tests cover initialization, migration and existing-destination preservation. Live tests are separate and require third-party services.

The `ndt/server.py` launcher delegates to `ndt/backend/server.py`; backend routing, persistence and drive-test services live under `ndt/backend/`. JavaScript unit tests, Python tests, browser tests and shared fixtures are separated under `ndt/tests/`.

Implementation references: `ndt/server.py`, `ndt/backend/`, `ndt/regions.json`, the modules in `ndt/dist/`, the OSM extraction scripts in `ndt/scripts/`, `ndt/tests/`, `Makefile`, and `ndt/README.md`. Data source dates and counts are described in the companion feature description; source links appear in the viewer README.

= Integrated DM architecture

`ndt/backend/server.py` mounts `ndt/dist/drive-test/` at `/drive-test/` and delegates `/api/dm/` requests to `DriveTestAPI` in `ndt/backend/dm/http.py`. Assets use relative or namespaced URLs and share the existing vendored MapLibre. API responses preserve the DM loopback Host, same-origin, request-size, content-type and input-validation checks. `--dm-db` selects the test registry. The default is `var/drive_tests.sqlite3`; if absent, startup copies the root `drive_tests.sqlite3` or the older `ndt/var/tests.sqlite3`. An explicitly selected planning database uses a registry beside it unless `--dm-db` is supplied. `--import-dm` uses the existing atomic SQLite backup/copy helper only when the destination is absent.

Planning and analysis databases remain separate. `regions.sqlite3` owns live Tasks, routes, speed cases, site memberships and static Virtual UEs. The DM registry owns captures, profiles, issues/activity, execution snapshots, association versions, experiments and comparisons. No historical test foreign key targets the mutable Task graph. `integration.catalog` opens the actual served planning database read-only in a consistent transaction and assembles context through `read_tasks`; it does not consult a hard-coded external checkout or a separate region JSON file.

