== Persistence and execution contracts

The `tests` table retains IDs, normalized compressed payloads and source hashes. A transactional table migration extends its origin constraint with `simulated` while keeping child references, profile/issue history and payloads intact. Existing profiles gain the additional planning-power threshold when read. `test_contexts` references the currently associated snapshot; `route_associations` retains immutable versions. `execution_snapshots`, `experiments` and `comparisons` persist frozen inputs, job progress/results and full comparison manifests. Comparison manifests embed the actual association basis, so changing a capture's active association cannot alter prior results.

Context identity includes Region/Project/Task IDs and names, routes, speed cases and eligible planning transmitters. `freeze_context` checks the expected context hash before freezing one selected route, geometry hash, route-quality/original-geometry flags, geodesic version and accepted inputs. New fitting retains and independently validates `originalCoordinates`; legacy lost input is not reconstructed. The route quality remains provisional or legacy-fit-unverified. Planning fits are not silently treated as road accuracy evidence.

The adapter resolves saved project membership or the existing nearest eligible seven-site default, using three usable bearing groups and reported frequencies/widths. Aggregated source frequency and beamwidth arrays are supported. One isotropic planning transmitter uses each eligible site's lowest usable reported frequency; an assumed RAT does not establish an actual live carrier assignment. Missing radio inputs prevent simulation instead of synthesizing New York reservation radio attributes.

Experiment submission validates IDs, supported parameter names, finite/ranged power and interval values, selected speed cases and transmitter membership. The immutable specification records baseline/changed EIRP, selected transmitter, mode, assumed RAT, sampling interval and cases. A server-generated frozen time origin makes repeated calculation deterministic. Retry with the same request ID and specification returns the same job; changed content returns a conflict. An immediate transaction also enforces the 20-job queue limit and concurrent retry uniqueness.

A POSIX advisory file lock elects one daemon worker per registry across local server instances. Only that owner recovers abandoned running work; other instances can still submit and inspect jobs. The worker atomically claims queued work. It generates positions by route chainage at constant speed, preserves exact endpoints and produces strictly increasing millisecond timestamps. Each complete run and its context are inserted in one transaction after checking cancellation. Progress records retain completed members. A/B compares each speed pair; only a fully successful experiment becomes completed. Startup marks abandoned running jobs interrupted and resumes queued work. Interrupted runs require a new submission. Cancellation checks use small status queries rather than loading growing result payloads.

`simulation.path_loss` matches the existing JavaScript UMa implementation's fixed heights, LOS/NLOS branches and distance/frequency domains. It evaluates the strongest supported planning transmitter and emits `predicted_rx_dbm`, planning identity and route chainage. It emits no fabricated RSRP, RSRQ, SINR, mobility outcome or service metric. Display fan geometry, terrain rendering, building shadows and pixel positions never feed propagation calculations.

== Alignment and comparison

Capture association preserves raw GPS and annotates each source observation by index/time/RAT, chainage, lateral error and status. Segment projection combines a 50 m lateral tolerance, 10 s continuity and forward/reverse monotonicity. Distant alternatives near intersections are ambiguous unless continuity resolves them. Missing/unmatched samples break continuous coverage. This version supports one ordered pass and makes no road-network matching or repeated-lap accuracy claim.

Comparison requires matching Task IDs, geometry and source origin. Controlled simulated pairs also require the same snapshot, case, model, mode, sampling and assumed RAT. Imported pairs require same-direction associations and remain observational. Metrics aggregate within 20 m bins; signal-power means use linear power. Every represented RAT needs at least 80 percent common bin coverage, additionally bounded by imported association continuous coverage. Insufficient coverage leaves captures accessible for descriptive analysis and returns an explicit error.

Saved comparisons retain both Test IDs and hashes, both observation sets, effective parameter contexts, association policies, bin/alignment versions, coverage, signed paired differences and the frozen evaluation profile. Analysis/experiment JSON contains this reproducible basis. The report's extra context sheet identifies route quality, model, speed, parameters and any saved comparison; generated hashes cover manifest and observations, whereas imported hashes identify source CSV bytes.

== Test driven development and browser verification

Integration contract tests were introduced before their implementations and observed failing for missing integration/HTTP contracts, ViewModel context injection, original-route retention, grouped source radio fields and retained comparison matching basis. The resulting tests cover stale launches, deterministic A/B, zero-change comparisons, concurrent idempotent submissions, cancellation, migration/preservation and route gaps/direction. Existing DM analytics and storage regressions remain part of `make check`.

Playwright includes the original analyst journeys and new Task-to-DM journeys against isolated disposable NDT/DM databases. These cover six-run speed matrices, saved-result reload, return navigation, measured route association/comparison, profile conflicts, source/import validation, sample findings/history, exports, catalog/storage recovery, sparse data, 100,000 observations, keyboard-accessible controls, axe checks and 320–1440 px geometry. Failed runs retain traces, screenshots and videos. `NDT_BROWSER` selects Chromium, Firefox or WebKit; installed browser availability and live external-map qualification are separate from the deterministic suite. Production databases are never used by these fixtures. Playwright is temporarily pinned to 1.63.0 because 1.65.0 exposes the #link("https://github.com/microsoft/playwright/issues/43967")[confirmed Firefox cross-world ARIA reflection crash]. A blank-page test also reproduces the Windows resize hang in 1.64.1. The retained regression checks named ARIA references through locators and native reads, axe with every rule retained, and post-audit resizing at 320, 390, 768 and 1440 pixels. Responsive assertions poll their existing exact-width or no-overflow condition after resizing. `integration.css` gives the drive workspace shell layout containment to prevent an obsolete overflow range after shrinking in WebKit; it does not clip content. The print rule restores `contain: none`, preserving multi-page reports. Browser checks also reject horizontal panning and verify the print override. Phone Task table and inventory review cases start in independent viewport contexts. On Windows, WebKit uses one Playwright project per spec, with one worker, to isolate browser graphics state between files. A long-worker trace recorded context loss and shader compilation failure before map readiness; no assertions, workflows or accessibility rules are skipped for this isolation. Task ID HTML patterns escape the hyphen for Unicode-set regular expressions; map and table editors reject punctuation and accept letters, digits, underscores and hyphens.

= Radio planning inspector and template design

`planning-model.mjs` owns pure site-label selection, RAT/bearing grouping, template validation and the single-link probe. `network-view-model.mjs` returns all enabled inventory markers independently of fan eligibility; invalid fan assumptions remove estimates without hiding inventory. `network-view.mjs` binds site facts and progressive disclosure to the DOM. Project Overview emits a site-review event, and the network view selects the site without needing a new network request. Project state supplies actual memberships and context through its existing view-model subscription.

`planning-label-view.mjs` owns MapLibre DOM labels. On map move, project/data change, selection or label toggle it removes old markers and rebuilds visible labels from current state. Project labels anchor at the first cluster member; selected sites have priority, followed by active-project members and other enabled sites. Screen-space collision rejection and a 45-item bound limit label clutter; regular site labels require zoom 13. Project and selected labels can overlap when necessary. No glyph service is required, and label text uses DOM text content.

`planning-view.mjs` binds explicit numeric fields to the pure model. The model rotates the probe direction into the downtilted element frame and applies TR 38.901 v18.0.0 table 7.3-1 horizontal/vertical attenuation, each capped at 30 dB and combined with a 30 dB cap. Its default peak gain is 8 dBi; gain changes create a customized element. The probe reuses `umaPathLoss` with fixed 25 m BS and 1.5 m UE. Peak EIRP equals conducted power plus peak gain minus feeder loss; receiver power subtracts pattern attenuation and path loss. Isotropic mode requires 0 dBi and ignores tilt. RAT and bandwidth are metadata and do not alter mean-power arithmetic. Blank/invalid fields clear the result and disable invalid exports. Valid templates can be saved even if the current probe is invalid.

Project scenarios use the SQLite revision store described below. Browser storage remains available for region-only studies; legacy project templates are recovered into an unsaved draft when no server scenario exists. Probe distance/bearing remain session inputs. Export JSON freezes the template target, probe, numeric results, context, saved history and pinned reference. The editable probe remains separate from map fans and project boundaries. Applied qualified templates can be explicitly selected for new Python virtual-drive and A/B calculations. Physical-cell assignment remains unavailable; explicit uniform-panel assumptions are supported as described below. Optional template carrier checks cover the pinned subset described below. See `docs/RADIO_PLANNING_ENHANCEMENT_PLAN.md` for priorities and acceptance criteria.

Browser fixtures resolve the base Python interpreter to avoid Windows venv launcher grandchildren. Individual fixtures terminate their owned child handle. The shared test server exposes a runtime identity only in its test wrapper; global teardown verifies the run ID and process ID over its live localhost connection before terminating it. Production endpoints are unchanged. This avoids process-tree enumeration, which is denied in the Windows test environment.

== Versioned project scenarios

Project studies can be named, saved, selected, copied and restored after reload. SQLite retains immutable template revisions, per-site template overrides, the regional source fingerprint, model version and creation time. Baseline and applied revision pointers remain fixed when a new draft revision is saved. A changed source fingerprint prevents applying an old revision until a new revision is saved. Apply saved revision records the immutable revision available for explicit selection in Drive tests. A newer saved draft does not change it. The probe uses editable inputs and map fans remain independent.

The browser distinguishes unsaved edits, draft revision, baseline and applied revision. Unsaved fields survive failed saves and project switches during the session. Concurrent or stale writes return a conflict without overwriting the current revision. Reload saved versions preserves a conflicting draft; Save as new scenario retains it under a new identity. Discard edits is explicit. A selected site can receive an assumed override, edited through Template target, without changing the default template. JSON export retains saved versions and labels unsaved edits. Legacy browser templates are recovered as unsaved drafts when the project has no server scenario.

The API is limited to same-origin localhost requests and validates complete template fields and known override site IDs. Transactions acquire an immediate write lock before comparing revisions. Limits are 20 scenarios per project, 50 retained revisions per scenario, 1000 site overrides and a 1 MB request. Scenario-library fingerprints identify an inventory version. A submitted experiment separately retains the complete contributing licensed records, regional metadata, optional original equipment file and applied scenario. Actual deployed-equipment assignment remains unavailable.

`radio_scenarios.py` owns validation, revision transactions and the two SQLite tables `radio_scenarios` and `radio_scenario_versions`. The current row stores baseline/applied pointers; version payloads are append-only through the API. `scenario-model.mjs` accesses the scoped HTTP endpoint. The pure `scenario-view-model.mjs` owns project isolation, unsaved draft buffers, request generations and optimistic revision requests. `planning-view.mjs` binds status, forms, history and recovery actions. No scenario is automatically assigned to a virtual-drive run.

== Equipment inventory design

`equipment_inventory.py` validates the normalized schema and cross-references against regional `network_sites`. It retains original UTF-8 bytes as a BLOB in `equipment_inventory_versions`, keyed by region, project and revision, alongside SHA-256, regional source fingerprint and import time. The source file itself retains equipment provenance and observation dates. Validation occurs before the insert under an immediate SQLite transaction. Expected-revision and preview-source checks prevent stale replacement; existing revisions remain unchanged. Empty or missing connections are never inferred from licensed records.

The scoped `equipment-inventory` endpoint supports GET for the current or a numbered historical revision, POST for a read-only preview and PUT to append an import. It uses the same-origin localhost guard. Invalid input returns 400, stale source/revision 409, unknown project/revision 404 and storage failure 503. Responses include parsed data, retained raw text and revision metadata; the 2 MB source and 4.01 MB request limits bound parsing and storage. Limits are 20,000 records total, 10,000 per collection and 50 project revisions.

`equipment-model.mjs` owns the HTTP requests. The pure equipment view-model retains project-scoped file drafts, suppresses late responses and requires a successful saved-state load and valid preview before commit. `equipment-view.mjs` renders import status, recovery, source downloads and the imported topology. The network inspector and New York reservation selection reuse that view. Graph traversal follows explicit cell/radio/port/antenna links; displayed missing fields remain distinct from zero. Pattern review plots the stored relative attenuation samples, with a labeled range for each cut. Sources use safe text nodes and HTTP(S) links. Patterns are not consumed by the propagation engine, and imported configuration has not been independently verified.

== Shared carrier validation design

The browser imports `carrier-bands.json` through `carrier-plan.mjs`; Python reads the same pinned JSON in `carrier_plan.py`. The file owns supported bands, operating ranges, channel offsets/steps, frequency raster, SCS, bandwidth lists and exact edition/reference links. Both validators check the optional template `carrier` object with `standard`, `band`, `channel` and `scsKHz`, and require its frequency to match the channel conversion within 0.000001 MHz. They reject unsupported plan versions, RAT mismatches, unsafe/non-integral channels, raster gaps, unsupported bandwidth/SCS and channels crossing a band edge. LTE B2/B4/B7 and NR n41/n77/n78 form a deliberately limited downlink subset; only NR 30 kHz SCS is supported. Full-channel containment is an additional conservative planner constraint.

`validateTemplate` calls the browser validator before probe computation. `validate_radio_template` calls the Python validator before storing any default or site override. The carrier object is optional for backward compatibility; old revisions are not silently upgraded. The planning view binds an explicit enable checkbox, derives read-only carrier frequency on channel edits, preserves saved qualification on load and exports plan/reference metadata. Invalid checked studies disable save/export/probe while generic studies stay labeled unchecked. The original immutable revision and optimistic-concurrency behavior is retained. Numeric bounds are checked before float conversion so oversized JSON integers produce validation errors in templates and equipment imports.

The shared table and Python/JavaScript boundary tests verify conversion, raster endpoints, SCS, bandwidth and edge rejection, including boolean, fractional, null and oversized numeric inputs. Browser tests cover inline errors, persistence/reload/export, LTE and NR, axe and 320-1440 px geometry. These are carrier-input checks, not certification of a complete 3GPP base station or a field-accuracy test. Explicit applied-scenario selection and qualified directional run gating are implemented below; reference-signal mapping and verified physical deployment remain unavailable; explicit imported cell/port assignment is described below.

== Frozen directional execution design

`integration._catalog` reads project radio context once per project and associates it with Task entries. Applied scenario descriptors contain applied/draft/baseline revisions, validation issues and compact derived transmitters. `freeze_context` starts one SQLite read transaction before loading the catalog, route, regional/equipment source records and scenario payload; concurrent writers cannot mix their versions in a snapshot. The context hash includes source, equipment and applied state, while the geometry hash remains route-specific. A stale selection returns a conflict. Directional snapshots use `ndt.execution.v2`; archived snapshots retain `ndt.execution.v1` semantics.

`scenario_transmitters` resolves the applied default and per-site overrides, validates templates and requires the pinned carrier qualification for used assignments. It produces one assumed carrier per eligible licensed Toronto site, retaining stable transmitter IDs and full templates. The engine filters by selected RAT. Complete contributing licensed features, regional metadata, explicit membership or automatic-default marker, original equipment bytes decoded as UTF-8 with BOM preserved, and the complete applied payload are frozen under `inventorySnapshot` and `radioScenario`. In legacy site mode, equipment imports remain provenance. Explicit source-cell mode below governs cell/path assignments; vendor cuts remain review-only.

`model_versions.py` pins legacy `uma-mean-power-fixed-heights-v1` and directional `uma-element-fixed-heights-v2` identities. `model_for` rejects unsupported or inconsistent frozen identities. `antenna.py` shares the probe's rotated reference-element attenuation and geodesic bearing behavior; Python/JavaScript parity covers boresight, halfwidth, rear and tilt vectors. Simulation validates frozen numeric templates without rechecking a newer live band table, applies per-template frequency/mode and subtracts directional attenuation from effective peak EIRP. Gain/loss are accounted once. Parameter A/B uses applied template EIRPs and changes one selected EIRP in B; saved-revision comparison is described below. Isotropic and archived runs preserve their behavior.

`ExperimentWorkspace` preserves explicit scenario choice, checks availability, derives RAT/targets, hides incompatible legacy controls and compares setup with the saved experiment specification. Request generations prevent late submissions/polls from replacing another Task; compact history records retain their outcomes. Result labels and reports use the frozen payload, not current controls. Reports retain exact model references and source hashes, show a compact assigned-template/power table and keep the comparison block together when printed. Numerical, transaction-isolation, immutable-source, queued-model and context-race tests accompany Playwright save/apply/run/report/export, axe and responsive checks in Chromium, Firefox and WebKit.

== Inventory review group design

`inventory_clusters.py` stores one revisioned JSON group list per region/project in `inventory_clusters`. Stable group IDs, unique plain-text names, current regional site IDs, list uniqueness and bounded assignment counts are validated before writes. An immediate transaction compares the expected revision and regional fingerprint; stale writers receive 409. GET reads source identity and groups in one transaction. Source refresh does not delete assignments; read responses explicitly list missing IDs. The scoped localhost GET/PUT API rejects cross-origin access and oversized requests. Groups do not mutate `project_sites`, equipment imports or execution templates.

`inventory-cluster-model.mjs` owns scoped HTTP requests. The pure `inventory-cluster-view-model.mjs` owns drafts, immutable accepted lists, base revisions, project request generations, filtering/sorting and geographic bounds. A reload can expose newer accepted groups while retaining the draft's older base revision; only Use saved groups discards it. Per-project draft buffers and identity-checked save cleanup prevent late replies from erasing newer edits. Membership filtering uses reported RAT metadata; no physical connectivity or operating state is inferred.

`inventory-cluster-view.mjs` binds the Overview table to the shared network/project ViewModels. It renders at most 25 rows, uses explicit local horizontal scrolling, and keeps group administration in expandable details. Ordinary controls expose search, RAT/scope/order, accepted group and concise status. Source text uses text content. Site review dispatches the existing selection event, and network selection highlights the matching table row. Visible planning membership buttons call the existing save-first project path; a data subscription refreshes project lists, map sources and boundaries after these external edits. Group metadata is independent of that Bell-only membership.

Saved group getters feed `planning-label-view.mjs`; labels contain project/name/count and use the selected group first. Separate vertical offsets distinguish group, project and site labels. A group map action fits coordinate bounds with controls padding; no RF boundary is calculated from those bounds. The inventory table is enabled for Toronto Bell/TELUS records; the storage API accepts known regional source IDs. Equipment reads now stage cleared bytes before asynchronous decode, guard replies by state identity and only rerender topology when accepted inventory changes. TDD covers concurrent storage, source changes, draft races, filter/bounds behavior, file failures and browser review/save/conflict/touch flows.

== Saved scenario comparison execution

The additional validated experiment type `scenario-ab` uses the same directional model identity and immutable execution snapshot contract. `scenario_baseline` resolves the library's baseline payload, checks current source identity, validates its model/templates/carriers and derives assignments through the existing per-site path. Catalog descriptors expose baseline availability/issues and RATs; the ViewModel intersects them with applied RATs. `freeze_context` retains full `radioBaseline`, `baselineTransmitters`, the existing applied payload/assignments and a compact `scenarioComparison` role summary. `scenario_changes` records selected-RAT parameter differences and marks metadata/isotropic-only orientation changes as recorded-only. Legacy site sides use current eligible project membership; source-cell sides below use their own explicit paths and current source records.

The worker selects baseline assignments for A and applied assignments for B, computes effective peak EIRPs independently and calls the pinned element or uniform-panel engine as identified in the snapshot. Each member retains its effective template set, original role summary and common parent snapshot identity. The original execution store retains the complete pair; saved comparisons retain both member contexts and observations. Single-transmitter EIRP B is applied only in `parameter-ab`. Queue/sample limits count two variants for either comparison. Worker-side experiment-type and snapshot-kind checks reject unsupported or inconsistent queued contracts before publishing members. Archived supported jobs keep their original model behavior. Cancellation responses remain tied to the originating Task and job, so switching Task during cancellation cannot publish an error or result into the new workspace.

The pure experiment ViewModel gates baseline qualification/common RAT, preserves scenario choice, counts the pair matrix and ignores unused legacy power/target/environment fields when judging setup differences for revision comparisons. The view hides those fields and reports frozen A/B revisions in results. `scenario-comparison-model.mjs` supplies safe text labels and preserves numeric zero versus missing assignment. Expandable result differences are capped at 100 visible rows and report differences at 20; complete inputs/differences remain in exports. Reports use the frozen role summary even for A, rather than mistakenly naming A's effective revision as applied B. TDD covers frozen orientation effects, later edits, identical revisions, missing/unqualified/outdated baseline, metadata-only zero effects, unsupported queued types, RAT intersection and formatting before browser run/export/report/accessibility checks.

== Equipment provenance in study templates

`equipment-study.mjs` resolves an explicitly selected cell/link through the normalized radio, ports, antenna, sector and regional site. It copies only the six reported fields, preserves nulls, selects the reference-element pattern and clears carrier qualification. Modeled carrier/port power, feeder loss and environment are inherited from project assumptions. `equipmentSource` contains exactly `revision`, `sha256`, `cellId` and `linkId`; pure browser/Python validators check its bounded revision, canonical hash and IDs. The optional field does not change the numerical prediction-model identity.

`equipment_studies.py` validates source references against immutable project imports and the override's inventory-site identity, reusing the importer trace path. A source default is rejected to prevent one physical path from being copied across an entire project. A scenario's source links must share one import revision. Save validates the historical path/hash; baseline/apply and run catalog checks additionally require the latest import and matching regional fingerprint. A project-scoped read cache avoids parsing the same source file for every saved scenario. The existing atomic execution snapshot retains original equipment bytes and the complete selected templates; the worker validates frozen metadata without consulting later live imports. Source-reference differences are recorded-only in revision comparisons.

The equipment view dispatches a source-selection event with project/site and immutable reference identity. The planning view waits for the current library load and checks its context generation before creating the override. The shared equipment getter supplies accepted source records without another fetch. The source card keeps identity and readiness above expandable reported/study values and a hash; it highlights stale links and reports missing inputs or modeled edits. Source comparison does not claim numeric equality or hardware conformance. Clearing a link retains explicit assumptions. Network/project changes refresh source context; target names use site labels. Port actions and source controls reuse the study button styles and remain usable in the bounded phone panel.

