== Uniform-panel array execution

Templates may select `pattern: array` with exactly six numeric `array` fields: `rows`, `columns`, `verticalSpacing`, `horizontalSpacing`, `steerAzimuthDeg` and `steerDowntiltDeg`. Browser and Python validators enforce whole-number dimensions 1-64, spacing 0.25-1 wavelength and local steering -90 to 90 degrees. Non-array patterns reject stale geometry. The controls use a bounded fieldset revealed by array selection, retain values across immutable revisions, and label gain per element. Source-to-study conversion clears inherited array geometry. Selecting array mode on a source-linked study clears reported antenna gain, requiring an independent element-gain assumption before saving.

For local direction components y and z, steering components ys and zs, rows M and columns N, the normalized power array factor is the product of squared normalized coherent sums in y/z: `abs(sum(exp(j*2*pi*n*dH*(y-ys)))/N)^2` times its vertical M/dV equivalent. Closed-form Dirichlet sums use a removable-singularity limit of one at integer phase cycles. This is equivalent to uniform complex weights normalized by `1/sqrt(M*N)`. The coherent peak factor M*N appears once in `peak_eirp`/`peakEirp`; normalized array attenuation and the mechanically rotated element attenuation are then subtracted. The output EIRP is an upper bound before element scan loss. Single-panel geometry and normalized electrical steering follow the scalar engineering subset of TR 38.901 section 7.3; this is not a complete polarized channel model.

The finite numerical array-null cap is 120 dB. The diagonal aperture uses the outer-element spans in wavelengths, and slant distance must meet `2*D^2/wavelength`; an inside-boundary transmitter is excluded. If no supported transmitter remains, the route sample records the reason without predicted power. New v3 route outputs below -300 dBm also remain unavailable to preserve the observation domain. The independent probe rejects an inside-boundary array. There is no near-field approximation, coupling, polarization mismatch, measured vendor reconstruction or coherent inter-port phase allocation.

Saved payloads containing an array use `uma-array-v2`; legacy payloads retain `uma-element-v1` and reject array reinterpretation. Freeze chooses `ndt.execution.v3` and `uma-uniform-panel-fixed-heights-v3` when either applied or baseline assignments contain arrays. It retains complete geometry, power convention, null cap, far-field rule and scalar limitations. The worker accepts v1/v2/v3 separately, validates frozen numeric inputs and uses the shared peak-power function in catalog, parameter A/B and revision A/B. V2 refuses arrays; legacy isotropic jobs retain their semantics. Geometry/steering differences affect the comparison; equipment-reference and bandwidth differences remain recorded-only. Reports format geometry and one-decimal upper-bound powers while exports retain precision. Execution summaries reuse the existing numeric formatter without changing editable or frozen power values.

The Windows Chromium test launch backports upstream Playwright fix 43605 for intermittent loopback socket allocation errors, preserving the pinned 1.63.0 default disabled-feature list and adding TcpPortRandomizationWin. This configuration does not change application code or user browser settings. Revisit it with the existing browser-upgrade regression checks when the temporary version pin can be removed.

TDD includes invalid shapes/bounds, booleans/fractional dimensions, coherent power normalization against an independent complex-sum oracle, boresight/null/steering/rotation, 1-by-1 equivalence, 60 JS/Python array vectors, far-field exclusion, low-output events, frozen jobs after later revisions and baseline/applied integration. Playwright covers geometry errors, save/reload/export, source gain review, real local experiment completion, report metadata, axe and 320-1440 px layout. These tests validate the implemented numerical contract and interface; they are not field calibration or hardware conformity evidence.

== Source-cell assignment and budget design

`radio_templates.py` holds shared numeric template validation. `cell_assignments.py` resolves exact imported cell/link paths with a project-scoped immutable source cache and rejects duplicated cells/physical ports, mismatched RAT/common cell-radio fields, mixed source revisions, non-one-to-one endpoints and overlapping occupied carriers on a shared TX port. Source mode is explicit `assignmentMode: cells`, with `cells` containing cell IDs and full per-link templates, and `radioBudgets` keyed by every assigned radio. Site overrides and source-linked defaults cannot mix with this mode. Bounds are 1000 cells, 64 ports/cell, 4096 paths and 4 MB; legacy site requests retain 1 MB.

`qualified_scenario` derives `CELL:` identities from imported cells and joins the actual regional inventory coordinates using Toronto site IDs or New York reservation IDs. It never substitutes Bell membership. A source-cell saved payload uses `uma-source-cells-v3`; freeze selects `ndt.execution.v4` / `uma-source-cells-fixed-heights-v4` whenever either comparison side uses cells. `sourceSites`, source-site kind and assignment modes replace the legacy-only source-location label. Complete original equipment bytes, revision/hash, both role payloads, port templates and cell/receiver/budget assumptions remain in the atomic snapshot. Old models reject port-bearing snapshots rather than silently reinterpret them; mixed comparisons adapt legacy sites as one-port cells inside v4.

For path powers p in dBm, `power_sum` uses a max-normalized logarithmic sum with stable summation. Every radio's conducted allocations include all assigned RATs/cells. Explicit unknown limits are saveable but not executable; finite -30 to 80 dBm budgets must cover the sum. Reported radio power does not set a modeled budget. Duplicate same-cell endpoints and unsupported RF sharing graphs fail before execution. Cell EIRP is the linear sum of port EIRP upper bounds, with a 0-90 dBm supported cell range. Parameter B adds one common dB delta to every target-cell port and checks per-port bounds and all-radio totals at submission and worker startup before any result member is published.

`simulate_cells` validates frozen numeric/template/path consistency without consulting a newer live carrier table. It computes the existing per-port fixed-height UMa path loss and isotropic/reference/array attenuation, adds received powers in linear units within each cell, then selects the strongest supported selected-RAT cell. All ports must meet the model domain/far-field requirement; output below -300 dBm is unavailable. Inter-port phases are uncorrelated with ideal polarization-insensitive power collection, an explicit study assumption rather than a full polarized/channel or receiver model. Arrays retain their coherent normalization only within a port. The job work gate counts observation-port evaluations, including both comparison variants, and rejects more than 20 million before queue insertion.

`cell-study.mjs` owns immutable source assignment, shared cell-radio propagation, explicit budget splitting/pruning and transactional source rebuilds. `cell-source-view-model.mjs` loads historical source revisions by exact revision/hash; project/source generation tokens discard stale successes and failures. `cell-study-view.mjs` renders a dedicated native dialog with 25-cell search/RAT pagination, explicit add/edit/remove actions, stable budget DOM inputs and one concise readiness issue. A sticky close header, Escape/focus recovery and 320-1440 px geometry keep the detailed work separate from the inspector. Source text uses text content. Source completion refreshes named target choices without losing the selected path; target options only rebuild when identities/labels change. Focus restoration uses the explicit opening control because WebKit does not always focus clicked buttons. Blocking cell/path and budget errors remain visible beside Save. Rebuild explains carrier/array/grid resets before acting. The existing template form edits one port; new-port defaults never execute. Inactive assignment-mode drafts are retained during editing rather than silently converted.

Historical assignments remain inspectable/saveable. Only a current source can apply, become baseline or run. Explicit rebuild copies current reported fields, clears carrier/array/grid assumptions and preserves modeled power/loss/environment plus surviving-radio budgets; absent paths fail without partial edits. Original revisions remain immutable. Source-cell probe JSON v5 (v6 with grids below) retains the complete current draft and source file; report tables cap path/radio rows at 20 and exports retain full coverage. Changes identify per-link inputs and recorded-only budget metadata. New York studies preserve reservation status and do not create operational-network claims. TDD covers independent mean-power arithmetic, budgets across RATs, source identity, obsolete models, work bounds, queued-source immutability, regional joins and browser history/edit/export/report/focus flows.

== Full-sphere source-pattern design

`pattern_models.py` and `pattern-model.mjs` implement the versioned scalar field independently. The format requires exactly the documented fields, real positive reference impedance and matched other ports. Strict increasing nonuniform axes cover the full sphere; seam/pole checks use 0.000001 dB tolerance. Gain bounds are -120 to 50 dBi. Validation caps individual and cumulative imported samples at 250000 before execution. Equipment schema v2 supports radiation-only records or paired relative cuts; per-antenna-port pattern IDs override the antenna fallback. Legacy v1 fields are never promoted. Original UTF-8 equipment bytes remain immutable.

Samples convert from dBi to linear gain. Piecewise trilinear interpolation uses actual axis intervals, wraps azimuth and forbids frequency extrapolation. For each frequency frame the implementation exactly integrates its piecewise bilinear power field over azimuth and cos(elevation)-weighted elevation; a stable small-angle formula avoids cancellation. Integrated efficiency above 1 plus 0.000001 rejects the grid without rescaling it. Positive real reference impedance and matched other ports make this passivity contract explicit; arbitrary complex power-wave normalization is unsupported. Frame maxima interpolate in linear power to produce a conservative frequency peak bound. Directional attenuation is bound dBi minus actual gain dBi, so gain enters the link budget once even when the bound is not attained.

`pattern_studies.py` / `pattern-study.mjs` bind exact source revision/hash/cell/link, declared antenna model, physical port name and selected pattern. A sampled template requires exactly `format`, `patternId` and mechanical `rollDeg` metadata and can exist only on an explicit source-cell path. Gain is derived and checked within 0.000000001 dB; occupied frequency coverage has 0.000000001 MHz endpoint tolerance. Carrier changes propagate within the cell and refresh every sampled port's independent gain. Electrical state is embedded; rigid azimuth/downtilt/roll rotation maps a global ray into the local field. Unknown explicit mechanical orientation stays invalid until reviewed; source rebuild drops grid/array metadata and carrier checks transactionally.

`pattern_context.py` selects unique qualification summaries and hashes, freezes them with the original equipment file, and prepares model assets once before worker publication. It rechecks original-byte SHA-256, schema, numerical summaries and actual physical source paths; floating summary equality uses 0.0000000001 tolerance. Full grids are not duplicated per port. Source-pattern library identity is `uma-source-patterns-v4`; a grid on either comparison side selects `ndt.execution.v5` / `uma-source-patterns-fixed-heights-v5`. Previous engines reject sampled inputs. Mixed site/array/cell comparisons use the same adapter with their original antenna behavior. Both A/B sides' frozen budgets/numeric templates are preflighted before any member is published, fixing partial A publication on invalid B inputs.

`antenna.py` and the independent probe use source assets for attenuation and the declared-dimension far-field gate `2*D^2/wavelength`. The route engine retains complete-cell gating, uncorrelated inter-port mean-power sums and fixed heights. Response is narrowband at carrier center, with full occupied-band coverage required; no band average is inferred. Scalar total power assumes ideal polarization-insensitive collection and supplies no receiver polarization, coherent inter-port phases or MIMO channel matrix. Numerical qualification and a declared measured/vendor label do not independently verify physical hardware or field accuracy.

`pattern-view.mjs` uses the existing native modal and sticky-close pattern. Project/target/source identity changes close stale review; source text is rendered as text content. Evidence, binding failure and preview are distinct: mismatched hardware remains inspectable while selection is disabled. Frequency/angle queries are independent of study carrier. A blue-to-yellow scale and labeled axes render a bounded heatmap; only display values use its floor. Detailed qualification/source records and evidence JSON are separate from primary actions. The editor labels mechanical orientation and read-only derived gain. Probe JSON v6, comparison formatting and the report model section preserve conventions, electrical state, frame efficiencies and hashes; report tables bound 20 models/24 frames while frozen JSON retains all data.

Independent closed-form interpolation/solid-angle tests, moving-lobe bounds, nonuniform axes, invalid conventions/passivity/sample limits, source binding, queued immutability and both-side preflight tests accompany 103 deterministic JavaScript/Python gain/rotation vectors. Browser workflows exercise LTE/NR save/reload/apply/run/report/export, mismatches, cut-only rejection, inert historic v1 fields, source rebuilds, keyboard recovery, axe and 320-1440 px layouts. These are software/numerical qualification tests, not field-accuracy evidence.

